 First, we need to find the volume of the pool:
Package: metrical
\documentclass{article}

\usepackage{amsmath, metrical}

\begin{document}

The volume $V$ of a rectangular prism with dimensions $L,$ $W,$ $H$ can be calculated using the formula $V = L \cdot W \cdot H.$ In the case of the swimming pool, $L = 25$ feet, $W = 14$ feet, and $H = 4$ feet.

\begin{align*}
V &= L \cdot W \cdot H\\
&= (25 \text{ feet}) \cdot (14 \text{ feet}) \cdot (4 \text{ feet})\\
&= \boxed{\text{2,300 cubic feet}}
\end{align*}

To convert from cubic feet to gallons, we make use of the fact that there are 7.48 gallons in a cubic foot:

\begin{align*}
V_\text{gallons} & = V_\text{cubic feet} \cdot \text{conversion factor}\\
&= 2,300 \,\text{cubic feet} \cdot \text{7.48 gallons per cubic foot}\\
&= \boxed{\text{17,088 gallons.}}
\end{align*}
The cost to fill the pool would then be:
\begin{align*}
\text{Cost} &= V_\text{gallons} \cdot \text{cost per gallon}\\
&= 17,088 \, \text{gallons} \cdot $0.10 \,\text{per gallon}\\
&= \boxed{\$1,708.80}
\end{align*}

\end{document}

